1
00:00:01.067 --> 00:00:04.471
In this lesson, you'll use the agentic document extraction framework

2
00:00:04.471 --> 00:00:06.873
from LandingAI to parse documents

3
00:00:06.873 --> 00:00:09.175
and extract key-value pairs

4
00:00:09.175 --> 00:00:11.277
without using character recognition,

5
00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:14.581
layout analysis, or vision language models.

6
00:00:14.881 --> 00:00:18.651
In the lab, you'll use ADE to parse complex documents

7
00:00:18.651 --> 00:00:21.654
and build a pipeline to parse mixed documents.

8
00:00:22.355 --> 00:00:24.357
All right, let's have some fun.

9
00:00:24.357 --> 00:00:28.361
This lesson is named a single API for agentic document extraction.

10
00:00:28.762 --> 00:00:30.864
So why did we choose that name?

11
00:00:31.031 --> 00:00:33.833
It's really a very apt description

12
00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:36.236
of LandingAI's Agentic Document Extraction.

13
00:00:36.736 --> 00:00:39.439
Much of the complexity you've seen in prior lessons

14
00:00:39.439 --> 00:00:42.242
is now replaced by a single API.

15
00:00:42.675 --> 00:00:45.178
So what are we going to cover in this lesson?

16
00:00:46.179 --> 00:00:49.482
After providing an overview of Agentic Document Extraction,

17
00:00:49.582 --> 00:00:52.185
we'll look at the two primary use cases,

18
00:00:52.585 --> 00:00:54.687
field extraction and RAG.

19
00:00:55.255 --> 00:00:57.657
Then I'll share some of the

20
00:00:57.657 --> 00:01:00.460
key technology differentiators that make ADE special.

21
00:01:01.061 --> 00:01:03.063
We'll look at one relevant benchmark,

22
00:01:03.463 --> 00:01:06.066
and then I'll set you up with some basics for getting started

23
00:01:06.433 --> 00:01:08.735
before you embark on the lab exercises.

24
00:01:09.436 --> 00:01:12.739
In my role as an applied AI engineer at LandingAI,

25
00:01:13.139 --> 00:01:17.243
I have the pleasure of helping our users build document intelligence pipelines

26
00:01:17.544 --> 00:01:19.646
around agentic document extraction.

27
00:01:19.879 --> 00:01:23.383
And I'll refer to it often by its acronym ADE.

28
00:01:24.184 --> 00:01:27.287
As this graphic suggests, ADE is an API

29
00:01:27.387 --> 00:01:29.989
that converts input documents, presentations,

30
00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.692
images, and even spreadsheets

31
00:01:32.792 --> 00:01:35.695
into well-structured Markdown and JSON.

32
00:01:35.962 --> 00:01:38.765
Our users include students, solopreneurs,

33
00:01:38.798 --> 00:01:42.469
small businesses, and large enterprises around the world.

34
00:01:42.969 --> 00:01:45.171
What are some of the key use cases?

35
00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:49.476
The first is field extraction, also known as key-value pair extraction.

36
00:01:50.176 --> 00:01:53.480
Users who receive a lot of user-supplied documents

37
00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:58.184
need the ability to extract specific information from those documents.

38
00:01:58.384 --> 00:02:00.887
and the ability to trace that back to the original.

39
00:02:01.588 --> 00:02:04.090
The second major use case is RAG,

40
00:02:04.491 --> 00:02:08.962
or building knowledge assistants that understand the underlying content.

41
00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:12.031
Users who prepare documents for RAG

42
00:02:12.031 --> 00:02:14.334
need the ability to understand content

43
00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:17.637
that also contains tables, figures, flowcharts,

44
00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:20.640
and the ability to trace those all back to the original.

45
00:02:21.141 --> 00:02:23.143
So, how does it work?

46
00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:27.447
ADE is not an incremental step over what existed previously.

47
00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:33.052
Rather, it's a novel approach to document AI, pioneered by our engineers.

48
00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:37.957
It's part of the agentic era with a Vision-First, Data-Centric approach.

49
00:02:38.358 --> 00:02:40.326
And these are the three pillars of ADE:

50
00:02:40.393 --> 00:02:42.929
Vision-First, Data-Centric, and Agentic.

51
00:02:43.363 --> 00:02:45.565
So, what do we mean by each of these?

52
00:02:46.065 --> 00:02:49.569
Vision-First means that we treat documents as visual objects

53
00:02:49.936 --> 00:02:54.541
where the meaning is encoded in the layout, structure, and spatial relationships.

54
00:02:54.941 --> 00:02:59.045
Data-Centric means training on the highest quality curated data.

55
00:02:59.445 --> 00:03:01.714
We believe that the right data is

56
00:03:01.714 --> 00:03:03.950
as important as the right model architecture.

57
00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:09.450
And finally, Agentic. Delivering systems that plan, decide, act, and verify

58
00:03:10.156 --> 00:03:12.559
until the response meets quality thresholds.

59
00:03:13.059 --> 00:03:15.862
So this graphic really drives home the point

60
00:03:15.862 --> 00:03:18.164
that vision is foundational to ADE.

61
00:03:18.565 --> 00:03:22.969
LandingAI brings years of vision expertise to the problem of document AI.

62
00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:28.274
The foundation layer at the bottom is state-of-the-art, Document-Native Vision Models

63
00:03:28.875 --> 00:03:30.877
that have been trained to see documents

64
00:03:30.877 --> 00:03:34.147
the way that humans see them. And built on that foundation

65
00:03:34.147 --> 00:03:37.550
are intelligent agents to handle Parsing &amp; Routing.

66
00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:39.919
Separate chunks such as text, tables

67
00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:42.455
and figures all follow a separate pathway.

68
00:03:42.655 --> 00:03:45.458
And finally, the AGENTS &amp; APPS layer on top.

69
00:03:45.658 --> 00:03:48.361
This layer focuses on what users really want

70
00:03:48.561 --> 00:03:50.730
in a document processing pipeline,

71
00:03:50.964 --> 00:03:54.734
delivering features such as field extraction and document splitting.

72
00:03:55.235 --> 00:03:58.004
And those foundational vision models from the prior slide

73
00:03:58.037 --> 00:04:02.041
are part of the growing Document Pre-trained Transformer family,

74
00:04:02.408 --> 00:04:04.010
or DPT for short.

75
00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:07.013
As of this recording, you can choose from DPT-1,

76
00:04:07.313 --> 00:04:10.016
DPT-2, and DPT-2-mini.

77
00:04:10.416 --> 00:04:13.119
All of them return high-quality document parsing,

78
00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:16.723
including reading order detection, layout detection,

79
00:04:17.023 --> 00:04:19.425
text recognition, and figure captioning.

80
00:04:19.926 --> 00:04:22.362
So how well do these DPTs

81
00:04:22.762 --> 00:04:25.098
at the core of ADE perform?

82
00:04:25.598 --> 00:04:28.801
Well, ADE actually exceeds human performance

83
00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:31.137
on the DocVQA benchmark set.

84
00:04:31.638 --> 00:04:34.140
And as presented in the table on the right,

85
00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:36.909
at 99.15% accuracy,

86
00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:39.946
it also exceeds all other published models.

87
00:04:40.413 --> 00:04:43.616
I'll draw attention to the blog post announcing these results,

88
00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:48.021
which has a wonderful interactive document gallery for you to explore.

89
00:04:48.321 --> 00:04:50.623
If you aren't familiar with DocVQA,

90
00:04:50.823 --> 00:04:54.827
it's question and answering benchmark of real scanned documents

91
00:04:54.927 --> 00:04:58.131
that comes from the UCSF Industry Documents Library.

92
00:04:58.231 --> 00:05:01.434
This slide shows as an example down in the lower right,

93
00:05:01.534 --> 00:05:03.703
the question to be answered is what

94
00:05:03.703 --> 00:05:06.239
is the home telephone number of the individual?

95
00:05:06.606 --> 00:05:09.409
And that answer can be found in the handwritten section

96
00:05:09.409 --> 00:05:11.210
in the green bounding box.

97
00:05:11.811 --> 00:05:14.514
So that brings us to the question, how do I use it?

98
00:05:15.014 --> 00:05:18.418
This is a screenshot from the ADE visual playground.

99
00:05:18.751 --> 00:05:22.255
ADE offers Parse, Split, and Extract separately,

100
00:05:22.655 --> 00:05:27.060
which you can flexibly combine when developing your document processing pipeline.

101
00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:31.364
Development options include the visual playground for drag and drop.

102
00:05:31.397 --> 00:05:33.499
the REST API

103
00:05:33.499 --> 00:05:35.902
and the Python and Typescript libraries.

104
00:05:36.202 --> 00:05:38.204
In the lab that follows,

105
00:05:38.204 --> 00:05:39.872
you'll only use the Python library

106
00:05:39.872 --> 00:05:41.874
and the Parse and Extract APIs.

107
00:05:42.208 --> 00:05:44.977
So be aware that Split is also available

108
00:05:44.977 --> 00:05:47.380
for splitting apart huge PDFs.

109
00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:50.983
And Parse Jobs is available for very large documents.

110
00:05:50.983 --> 00:05:53.586
Think, you know, hundreds to thousands of pages.

111
00:05:53.986 --> 00:05:55.988
And to use ADE outside of the course,

112
00:05:56.389 --> 00:05:58.191
you'll need your own API key.

113
00:05:58.691 --> 00:06:01.994
You can generate a free key and get some initial free credits

114
00:06:02.195 --> 00:06:03.996
at va.landing.ai.

115
00:06:04.797 --> 00:06:06.799
And now it's lab time again.

116
00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:10.002
Join me for three hands-on exercises.
